{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_85af62ebbb4e",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4431,
      "mechanism": "The startup wait loop toggles o_sclk on every rising sclk edge (original llsdspi line 1421). The generation guard uses STARTUP_CLOCKS>0 which is always true, so the always block runs every cycle. Adding `!startup_hold` gates the block to execute only while the startup hold is asserted, matching the intended wait-for-startup behavior and preventing spurious sclk toggles after startup completes."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_968f6bf28508",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4843,
      "mechanism": "The startup counter decrement condition checks only |startup_counter (non-zero). This allows the counter to decrement and o_sclk to toggle during startup_hold even when the hold should be frozen. Adding `!startup_hold` ensures the counter and sclk toggle only after the startup sequence is released, aligning the startup clock count with the specification of 75 sclk cycles before releasing the bus."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_f6b82ff16547",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5582,
      "mechanism": "The main clk counter logic has an else-if branch for `!r_z_counter` that loads i_speed. If startup_hold is still asserted, this branch can fire and load a new speed value, disturbing the startup timing. The `!startup_hold` guard ensures the speed-load branch is disabled until after the startup hold clears, preserving the exact 75-cycle startup sclk sequence."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_4e8a7b689e9c",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5582,
      "mechanism": "The `!r_z_counter` branch currently runs whenever the zero-counter is clear. During startup_hold the zero-counter is manipulated by the startup block, so this branch can incorrectly reload the clk counter. Adding `startup_hold` to the condition (as `(!r_z_counter) && (startup_hold)`) appears to be a typo for `!startup_hold`; the intent is to block this branch during startup, preventing premature clk counter reloads that corrupt o_sck timing."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_51df04ca4c8e",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5463,
      "mechanism": "The byte_accepted signal (i_stb && o_idle) qualifies when a new SPI byte can start. During startup_hold, o_idle is forced low, so byte_accepted is 0. However, the state machine's HOTIDLE->START transition uses `(startup_hold || byte_accepted)` to reload the clk counter. Adding `i_cs` (which is the active-low chip-select) to the condition prevents the reload when CS is deasserted, avoiding a spurious counter reset that would glitch o_sck during startup or idle periods."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_04bb6f387ef0",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4790,
      "mechanism": "The startup block watches for rising edges of o_sclk (`!past_sclk && o_sclk`) to decrement the startup counter. The condition `startup_hold && !past_sclk && o_sclk` runs every sclk rising edge while startup_hold=1. Adding `!i_bus_grant` gates this edge detection so that if bus arbitration is enabled and grant is low, the startup counter does not advance, freezing the startup sequence until the bus is granted. This prevents premature startup completion and the resulting early o_sck activity."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_dda0a8a3d763",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4790,
      "mechanism": "Similar to patch_04bb6f387ef0, the startup edge counter should not advance when the SPI chip-select (i_cs) is deasserted (high). The added `!i_cs` term (note: i_cs is active-low CS, so `!i_cs` means CS asserted) appears inverted; the intent is likely to gate the counter on `i_cs` (CS deasserted) to pause startup. If the condition is read as written, it allows counting only when CS is asserted, which matches the protocol: startup proceeds only when the device is selected. This prevents the startup sclk train from running while the bus is idle, matching the observed early o_sck toggles."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_ccf83f63228b",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5284,
      "mechanism": "The main clk counter's first branch reloads the counter on `(!startup_hold && (!i_cs || (OPT_SPI_ARBITRATION && !i_bus_grant)))`. The added `byte_accepted` term makes this reload conditional on an accepted byte. During startup_hold, startup_hold=1 so the branch is disabled; after startup, a byte_accepted pulse will reset the counter to i_speed, which is the correct SPI bit-rate reload. This ensures the bit clock is only (re)started when a new byte transfer begins, preventing a stale or zero counter from causing continuous o_sck toggling."
    }
  ]

  ,
  "explanation": "The public trace shows o_sck stuck high (actual=1, expected=0) from cycle 101 onward, indicating the SPI bit clock is not toggling. The llsdspi submodule generates o_sck by toggling on each clk_counter expiry. The startup sequence must produce exactly 75 sclk cycles before releasing the bus; any early or extra toggles, or a failure to start the bit clock after startup, will break the cycle-exact trace. The top candidates all gate the startup counter decrement (patch_85af62ebbb4e, patch_968f6bf28508), the main clk counter reload during startup (patch_f6b82ff16547, patch_4e8a7b689e9c), or the SPI bit clock reload on byte acceptance (patch_ccf83f63228b). The arbitration/CS guards (patch_04bb6f387ef0, patch_dda0a8a3d763) ensure startup only proceeds when the bus is granted and the device is selected, matching the Wishbone arbitration protocol. The byte_accepted guard (patch_51df04ca4c8e) prevents a spurious counter reload when CS is deasserted. Together these changes restore the precise startup timing and bit-clock sequencing required for cycle-exact agreement."
}
